  "n_resamples": 10000,
  "question": "Which descriptor block, and how far it reduces.\nA gate is a judgement, made by a person and recorded with its reason, not an algorithm. At the top of these tables the configurations are statistically tied, so any tie-break is a preference: cheapness, fewer encoders to train, less memory. Expressing a preference as a cost ordering does not make it less of a preference, it only makes it harder to argue with, and it invites tuning the ordering until it returns the answer already believed. So the pipeline measures and a person decides.\nWhat is measured, and recorded with every decision: each configuration scored by the mean of its seeds, with the spread across them and the seed ensemble alongside; every pair compared by a paired bootstrap over the 260 compounds, resampled on one shared set of draws so each difference is paired, with the family corrected by Benjamini-Hochberg at a false discovery rate of 0.05; and the cost facts, being blocks joined, encoders to train, and columns carried.\nThe score is the mean over seeds rather than the ensemble of them, because the subject is how well a single model does. Seeds are replicates, for statistical power and a variance estimate, not a way to build a better predictor. The ensemble is carried because the leaderboard anchor is one, and is the only quantity comparable to it.\nTwo limits the correction does not remove. Benjamini-Hochberg assumes independence or positive regression dependence, and comparisons on a shared compound set are correlated rather than independent; that is the case it is generally taken to cover, and it is not proven here. And the reference is the empirical leader, chosen by looking at the same data the comparisons use, so a tied set is if anything narrower than it should be.\n",
